# J0(3M)

## NAME

j0, j1, jn, y0, y1, yn, erf, erfc - bessel and error functions

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <math.h>

double j0(double x);

double j1(double x);

double jn(int n, double x);

double y0(double x);

double y1(double x);

double yn(int n, double x);

double erf(double x);

double erfc(double x);
```

## DESCRIPTION

These functions calculate Bessel functions of the first and second kinds for real arguments and
integer orders.

**Note:** *erf* and *erfc* are documented here because they have no page of their own and the
two families share the same provenance and the same fits. *Erf* returns the error function of
*x*; *erfc* returns 1 - erf(x), computed directly rather than by that subtraction - for large
*x* every significant digit of the result lies in the digits that would cancel. Both are v7
extensions and not C11. v7's <math.h> declared them and this tree's silently did not, which was
an oversight: the sources were always there.

## SEE ALSO

exp(3), floor(3), intro(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Non-positive arguments cause *y0, y1,* and *yn* to return -HUGE_VAL and set *errno* to EDOM.
**Note:** v7 said "negative"; zero is a domain error too, and the value returned is the most
negative finite value this machine has, not a -infinity it has not.

*J0, j1, jn, erf* and *erfc* are finite everywhere and have no error return.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The leading coefficients did not fit, so they are scaled

This is the whole of the port for these two files. v7's rational fits for *j0* and *j1* open
with coefficients near 5e20 to 5e23, and the largest finite value on this machine is 9.223e18 -
**so they cannot be written as literals at all.**

Each numerator/denominator *pair* is divided by a power of ten (10**10, 10**11 or 10**13, noted
on each array's first line), which is a pure shift of every literal's decimal exponent and
leaves the ratio n/d - the only thing either polynomial contributes - unchanged. The pairs that
already fit are v7's outright.

The Horner loops needed no unrolling: *b6cc* emits an initialized file-scope **double** array
to the constant pool.

### gamma is absent

v7's *gamma* is the one routine of its libm whose source never reached this tree. Nothing here
calls it, it is not C11, and <tgmath.h> does not name it either. It comes back with the first
program that wants it, and the choice between v7's log-gamma-plus-signgam and C11's
tgamma/lgamma gets made then.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libm.a
| lib/libm/j0.c, j1.c, jn.c, erf.c
